WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.600
Becoming a billionaire is hard.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:09.540
As of April 2021, there were only a total
of 2755 billionaires in the entire world meaning

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:15.740
that only 1 in every 2.8 million people reach
the elusive 10 figure mark.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.750
Given the difficulty of the task, it’s not
surprising that most billionaires spend their

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.900
entire careers working towards 10 figures
with the average billionaire taking 21 years

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.470
to reach the mark.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.770
Even the most notable billionaires in history
including Bill Gates, Steve Jobs, and Elon

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.940
Musk took over a decade to make their first
billion.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.210
One entrepreneur named Jay Walker, however,
managed to accomplish this feat within just

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.920
12 months which is the fastest in history.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.899
So, here’s how Jay Walker made $1 billion
in 1 year.

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:51.770
Taking a look back, Jay Walker was born to
an immigrant family on November 5, 1955, in

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:52.770
Queens, New York.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:57.190
His mother’s family had fled to New York
from Germany right before the start of World

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.250
War 2.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.559
Meanwhile, his father’s side of the family
had lived in the US for a few generations

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:06.060
and even made some good money during the roaring
20s.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.930
From a very young age, Jay found himself embarking
on endeavor after endeavor trying to make

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.920
a bit of money here and there.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.990
He sold candles, seeds, and jelly door to
door, and he delivered newspapers to his community.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:23.860
As he grew older, Jay became more involved
in the community, joining student government.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:29.079
Nowadays, student government is more or less
just a resume padder, but it seems like back

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:32.990
in the day, Jay actually played an active
role in the community.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:37.020
One time, he was the student representative
in a debate between the teacher’s union

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.860
and the city of Yonkers during a funding crisis.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:45.490
Anyway, Jay ended up graduating from high
school in 1973, and he would go on to attend

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.920
Cornell University where he majored in industrial
relations.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.890
Jay later revealed that the motivation behind
getting this degree was to hopefully score

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.930
a spot as an assistant to an extremely wealthy
individual.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.400
Jay strongly believed that understanding how
wealthy people operated was essential in becoming

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.110
wealthy himself.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.260
Jay didn’t stick around at college for too
long though as he would drop out and try to

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.399
start a business.

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:10.680
Unfortunately, though, this didn’t go too
well.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.099
Jay started a free weekly newspaper in Ithaca,
New York.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:20.090
Things started off great as Jay quickly grew
to 175 employees with 25,000 newspapers in

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:21.090
circulation.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.459
However, his business would crumble when the
local daily paper company came out with their

00:02:25.459 --> 00:02:27.060
own weekly newspaper.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:32.930
Jay shut down his newspaper business and returned
to college shortly after, but now he had $150,000

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.819
in debt or $675,000 today.

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:42.830
Jay would graduate in 1977 and get a job at
a magazine company called Folio after college.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:47.390
He worked on the marketing side of Folio for
about four years before he quit and tried

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:50.269
to start another company called Visual Technologies
Corporation.

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:54.090
The company sold glass sculptures that emitted
light whenever someone touched them.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.760
But, while these sculptures had some novelty,
they didn’t really serve much of a purpose.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.310
Jay also quickly found out that the sculptures
were extremely expensive to manufacture.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:06.950
Just a few years after starting the company,
Visual Technologies Corporation would file

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.810
for bankruptcy in 1986, having lost a painful
$5.3 million.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.620
Jay didn’t quit though, he instantly switched
to running another business called Catalog

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.120
Media Corporation.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.860
Jay partnered with Trans World Airlines to
offer discounted flight tickets to readers

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.010
of the catalog.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:27.420
Jay actually managed to sell a large amount
of discounted tickets; however, there was

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.510
a problem.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.790
Jay had been selling the discounted tickets
to travel agents as opposed to end consumers

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.269
which was in violation of the contract with
Trans World Airlines.

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:40.180
Trans World Airlines would sue Catalog Media
Corporation, and this company would also be

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.370
shut down in 1988.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:47.060
At this point, Jay was only 33 years old,
but he had already run 3 businesses into the

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.739
ground losing millions of dollars and destroying
his credit.

00:03:50.739 --> 00:03:54.060
Nonetheless, Jay still wasn’t ready to give
up.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:58.900
In 1991, Jay would form a joint venture with
one of his biggest investors over the years

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.209
Michael Loeb.

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:04.580
This business was called NewSub Services,
and it specialized in selling magazine subscriptions

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.350
through credit card companies.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:10.550
The company didn’t skyrocket or anything,
but for the first time in Jay’s career the

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.459
company also didn’t shut down.

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:18.150
In fact, this company is still around 30 years
later, and they go by the name Synapse Group.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:24.610
They employ about 170 people today so nothing
super insane, but also nothing to scoff at.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.889
Jay could’ve stuck around at Synapse Group,
and he would’ve done just extremely well,

00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:31.020
but Jay had even larger ambitions.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.150
So, in 1995, he would start yet another company
called Walker Digital.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:41.170
Jay was a massive fan of Thomas Edison, and
he wanted this company to basically be a digital

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:43.919
version of Thomas Edison’s laboratory.

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:48.590
At first, Jay dabbled with some software that
could be used in restaurants, but soon enough

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:53.130
Jay would land on his billion-dollar idea:
buyer-driven pricing.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.370
Buyer-driven pricing is when consumers tell
companies how much they’re willing to pay

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:01.400
for a service or product, and then, the companies
will do business with customers with reasonable

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:02.470
offers.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:06.620
This business model was especially attractive
within the airline industry.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.169
Consumers often wanted discounted airline
tickets, and airlines were often willing to

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:13.120
get rid of empty seats for below-market prices.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.930
Spotting this massive opportunity, Jay went
ahead and applied for a patent for the business

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:18.100
model.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.240
And fun fact, this was the first time that
the US Patent Office granted a patent for

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.540
a business method.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:29.480
After receiving his patent, Jay founded a
company called Priceline.com in April of 1998.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.840
Jay had partnered with a plethora of different
airlines, but despite that, during the first

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.590
year of operation, only about 7% of customers
were actually able to buy a ticket through

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:39.590
Priceline.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:42.530
Honestly, though, this wasn’t that big of
a deal.

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:46.550
If you were planning on traveling somewhere,
you could make a bid for a flight ticket on

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:47.550
Priceline.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.870
If you were lucky, you’d get a discounted
ticket.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.730
If not, you can just buy a ticket at market
price.

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:57.330
In other words, there was no reason to not
give Priceline a shot, and this made Priceline

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.170
extremely popular amongst consumers.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:04.900
Despite this monumental demand, Priceline
was losing a lot of money, but given that

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.240
investors were more than happy to back internet
companies at the time, their financial shortfall

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.450
wasn’t that big of a deal.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:16.391
In April of 1999, one year after founding
the company, Jay took Priceline public, and

00:06:16.391 --> 00:06:19.660
as you would guess, the stock exploded.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:26.169
Priceline ran up to a valuation of $11.8 billion
and Jay’s stake ballooned to $5.2 billion.

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:30.910
Jay had finally made it as an entrepreneur,
and so, he decided to celebrate.

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:35.680
Jay bought himself a third Mercedes and he
started construction on his dream mansion.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.199
Jay didn’t get complacent though, he continued
to aggressively expand Priceline.

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:44.530
For example, Jay worked quite hard to get
actor William Shatner on board.

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:49.250
At first, William didn’t want to meet with
Jay at all, so Jay agreed to pay William to

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:50.840
have a meeting with him.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.680
During the meeting though, William would become
so impressed with Jay that he agreed to do

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.700
the ads for Priceline for stock compensation
as opposed to cash compensation.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:02.800
Jay also tried to expand the business model
to groceries and gas as well with the launch

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:04.449
of Priceline WebHouse.

00:07:04.449 --> 00:07:08.370
Though there was a large amount of demand
from consumers for this service, the logistics

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:11.099
of running this business were a nightmare.

00:07:11.099 --> 00:07:15.020
But little did Jay know that this was the
least of his concerns.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:19.610
In early 2000, the dot-com bubble burst, and
valuations of all internet and tech companies

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:20.840
fell off a cliff.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.009
Jay wasn’t phased by the crash at first
though.

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:27.020
He was like, I’ll just buy the dip at these
discounted prices and hold until the stock

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.020
recovers.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.780
Unfortunately, though, Jay wasn’t buying
the dip.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.800
He was buying a downtrend.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.610
To make things even worse, 9/11 would kill
consumer demand for airline tickets, and this

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.949
would push Jay over the edge.

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:44.810
At the end of 2001, Jay would shut down Priceline
WebHouse, and sell his entire stake to Hong

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:45.880
Kong investors.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.610
In retrospect, Jay literally sold at the bottom.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.479
I’m really not sure why he did this.

00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:57.620
He could’ve sold when he was down 50%, 60%,
even 80%, and he could’ve stilled walked

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.380
away a billionaire.

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:03.570
But, he decided to sell when he was down 94%.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:07.919
On the bright side, this still came out to
about $333 million after taxes.

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:13.080
But, when you’re down 94%, you might as
well weather out the storm and stage a recovery.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.880
I know that’s easy to say in hindsight,
but many entrepreneurs like Jeff Bezos and

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.110
Steve Jobs did exactly that and it worked
out extremely well for them.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:24.620
Given the resilience Jay showed in the 70s
and 80s by starting business after business,

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:30.290
this decision is quite confusing, but whatever
the reasoning, this is what Jay ended up choosing.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:35.460
Anyway, after the dot-com crash, Priceline
stock didn’t really move for another 5 years.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:40.770
Priceline started to make a recovery in 2006
and 2007, but they got crushed once again

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.019
by the 2008 financial crisis.

00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:48.200
It wasn’t until 2009 that Priceline really
started to grow once again, but they haven’t

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.200
looked back since.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:54.930
In the 2010s, Priceline went on a buying spree
acquiring everyone from Kayak and OpenTable

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.279
to HotelsCombined and Venga.

00:08:57.279 --> 00:09:01.370
At the end of 2013, Priceline finally broke
out of their dot-com peak, and they have over

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.990
doubled since then.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:08.160
In 2018, the Priceline Group changed their
name to Booking Holdings and now they sit

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.830
at a valuation of $94 billion with a total
of 19,400 employees.

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:19.290
Jay had a 35% stake in the company, and even
if we say that stake was watered down to 20%,

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:23.390
Jay would be worth a little under $20 billion
today if he held on.

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:27.550
After leaving Priceline, Jay returned to Walker
Digital, and he’s been working there ever

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:28.560
since.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.649
As we previously discussed, Walker Digital
is more of a research company as opposed to

00:09:32.649 --> 00:09:34.060
consumer business.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:38.980
So, Walker Digital mainly works in the background
licensing technology and inventions to companies

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:40.600
like Time Warner.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.980
Given that Walker Digital doesn’t actually
sell any products themselves, many consider

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.610
the company to be a patent troll.

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:51.100
Given that Jay Walker is the 10th most patented
living inventor today, I don’t think that

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.350
title is necessarily inaccurate.

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:59.070
Today, Jay Walker is estimated to be worth
about $500 million, so it doesn’t look like

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:03.779
Jay ever recovered the money he lost during
the dot-com crash, but I don’t really think

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:05.480
he cares to even do so.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.149
At the end of the day, Jay was able to go
from 0 to over a billion dollars within a

00:10:09.149 --> 00:10:14.140
single year thanks to the explosive success
of Priceline combined with the dot-com bubble.

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:18.269
Though most of Jay’s wealth was made within
an extremely short period of time, the truth

00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:22.730
is that Priceline’s success was a culmination
of decades of repeatedly starting businesses,

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:24.880
failing, and trying again nonetheless.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.649
So, I personally think Jay’s eventual success
was well deserved even though there was a

00:10:29.649 --> 00:10:32.580
good amount of luck mixed in with his final
rise.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:33.700
Do you guys agree?

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:35.000
Comment that down below.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.910
Also, drop a like if you respect Jay’s unwavering
resilience throughout the late 1900s.

